<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Early modern period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–<a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Early modern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The early modern period is a historical period of about 1500 to about 1800, defined chiefly from the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as the time after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and before the advent of modernity; its boundaries are not agreed. Proposed starting points include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> revolution of the 1440s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">Fall of Constantinople</a> in 1453, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and its end is tied to the Industrial, American and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolutions</a>. Historians have stressed the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. The medieval historian Lynn Thorndike first proposed the term for a distinct period in 1926.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>European history has traditionally been divided, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanists, into ancient, medieval and modern periods. Historians occasionally used the term early modern in the early twentieth century, the first known use dating to 1895, but mostly for the early centuries of the modern period and not for a period with defining features.</p><p>Lynn Thorndike proposed it as a distinct period in his Short History of Civilization of 1926, as a broader alternative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Economic historians took it up in the 1940s and 1950s, and it became widely known among scholars during the 1990s. The period has no exact beginning or end. It is generally held to have started at the beginning of the sixteenth or seventeenth century and to have ended in the eighteenth or nineteenth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> feudalism declined, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a> ended and the religious unity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> under the Roman Catholic Church broke up. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> caused a backlash that expanded the Inquisition and sparked the wars of religion, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, which ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. The period also saw the colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the Commercial Revolution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a> and the Great Divergence, in which Western Europe came to surpass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in technology and per capita wealth.</p><p>Elsewhere the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> rose from 1526, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-confederacy">Maratha Confederacy</a> became the dominant power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1740 until its expansion was halted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761. By the end of the period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> were mostly closed to Europeans, though trade continued in ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Historians in recent decades have argued that, from a worldwide standpoint, the most important feature of the period was the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a>. On this view the Columbian contact, which added a New World to the Old, is another marker of its start. New economies and institutions became more globally articulated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercantilism">mercantilism</a> came to dominate economic theory.</p><p>The transition to the late modern period in the late eighteenth century was marked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, followed by the second wave of modern colonisation known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-imperialism">New Imperialism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, called the Age of Reason, departed from scholasticism and put reason at the centre of knowledge.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Early modern period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
